WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.240 --> 00:00:03.270 align:start position:0%
 
it's daybreak  and hector carrillo is on his way to  milk his cows  he follows a path up the nambiugua a  mountain in the la sepultura biosphere  reserve in southern mexico  legend has it that anyone who tries to  take something away from the mountain  will never return home  [Applause]  the slopes are shrouded in an almost  primal mist  there's been widespread deforestation on  many of the other mountain slopes in the  region  farmers keep their cattle in the forests  that remain  hector carillo's cows graze on land  that's fenced off to protect the  woodland  wreak havoc in the forest they destroy  everything contaminate the streams  and cause ground erosion so if they run  around the forest and it rains  there'll be landslides  hector keeps 20 cows he used to have  many more but he got rid of half of them  because he knows that traditional  livestock farming is bad for the  environment  decades ago his grandfather switched  from growing corn and beans to livestock  farming  because it was more profitable  three years ago hector joined the  project bio pasos  which promotes cattle farming that's  sustainable and doesn't destroy  diversity  his income is starting to increase and  the environment is gradually recovering  but it's been a difficult few years  but i asked him to give me a chance  when i took the risk of my small farm i  went through some hard times  very hard times i milked my cows  constantly but the yields were low  indigenous farmers traditionally let  their livestock graze in forest pastures  and also grew vegetables their methods  were sustainable  their expertise had largely been lost  now their descendants are looking to  revive it and save the cloud forest  jose antonio jimenez coordinates the bio  pasos project in the state of chiapas  meticulous planning facilitates the  farmers switch to the climate smart  livestock concept  jimenez knows the region well and can  provide practical support when necessary  the biggest challenge he faces is  convincing the farmers to  change the way they've always done  things  we need to work with the farmers to  identify problems together  we also need to work with them to find  solutions  this process taking into account the  expertise and experience of the farmers  and their families  helps them to learn new things we must  always factor in what the farmers know  protection through production that's the  motto of bio pasos  farming practices don't have to entail  forest degradation  some 1 200 farmers in the region have  joined the initiative  most of them are cattle farmers  [Applause]  juan pimentel still has a herd of cows  but he's also cultivating bananas and  soursop trees on a section of his land  even though it'll be five years before  the fruit provides him with a solid  income  he signed up with bio pasos  people think we're crazy they don't know  why we're planting small trees when we  won't even live to see them grow  but my wife and i hope that even if we  never get to harvest the fruit  one day our children and our  grandchildren will benefit  and they'll also benefit from improved  air quality  a year ago juan pimentel's wife julieta  was one of several local women in the  village los angeles  to found their own cheese dairy which  they run as a cooperative  they all used to make cheese at home by  themselves  now they've joined forces  the equipment is all brand new and was  provided by b  o pasos the women produce  five varieties of cheese  we women talk a lot we say what we think  it's good that we're making different  kinds of cheese  everyone knows different varieties so  we're experimenting  we women support one another  the farming families used to sell their  milk via a middleman  at a very low price now they're earning  better  that was one of the main incentives for  joining the project  and switching to sustainable production  it allows them to diversify it means  they can sell their produce at higher  our prices was not only to teach them  new technical skills but to support them  in the whole production chain  from the farm to the consumer  hector and rosa carrillo have also  invested in a cheese  press it's helped speed up the  production process  and they hope that in the long run it  will secure them a steady income  you

